Based on the search results, the 'BlueEfficiency' branding was used by Mercedes-Benz on previous generations (like the W212 E-Class, produced 2009-2016) to denote models with specific fuel-saving technologies. These technologies included things like aerodynamic improvements, low rolling resistance tires, optimized energy management (like regenerative braking via the alternator), and efficient engines, often featuring start/stop functionality. Mercedes claimed significant fuel savings (over 20%) for these models compared to their predecessors.

However, Mercedes-Benz no longer uses the distinct 'BlueEfficiency' branding on its *most recent* models, including the current W214 E-Class (launched in 2023 for the 2024 model year). The efficiency technologies developed under the BlueEfficiency initiative are now integrated into the standard technology stack across the lineup. Modern E-Class models (W214) feature mild-hybrid (MH) or plug-in hybrid (PH) technology as standard across various engine options.

Since the request is strictly for the *most recent version* of the 'BlueEfficiency' model, and this specific branding/trim does not exist for the current W214 E-Class, I will evaluate the base model of the latest generation (W214). This represents the modern equivalent, incorporating standard efficiency technologies. I'll use the E 200 (available in Europe) as the representative base model, featuring a 2.0L 4-cylinder mild-hybrid engine.

